# Line endings are normalized in the repository, and this is not cosmetic.
#
# scripts/build-wails.sh was committed with CRLF throughout, so its shebang was
# really "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r" and the release workflow's macOS leg — which
# runs ./scripts/build-wails.sh — died with "bad interpreter" before building
# anything. CI never caught it because the only check on that file is
# `bash -n`, which parses a CRLF script happily.
#
# The working tree had no single convention either: the same extension appeared
# in both flavours (.ts was 83 CRLF / 19 LF, .go 21 / 133), which is what turned
# ordinary edits into whole-file diffs and made `gofmt -l .` fail on a Windows
# checkout for files nobody had touched.
# The pattern and its attributes have to share a line: split across two lines,
# "*" becomes a pattern with no attributes and the rule silently does nothing.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows reads these with tools that still expect CRLF, and a Windows editor
# that rewrites one to LF produces a whole-file diff for a one-line change.
# Both files carry a UTF-8 BOM, which is content: this does not remove it.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary: never normalized, never diffed or merged as text.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.node binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.sqlite3 binary
